1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,809
Ethan, I think two polite words made my friend believe I was secretly annoyed with her.

2
00:00:07,809 --> 00:00:12,929
That is impressive. Which two words did the damage?

3
00:00:12,929 --> 00:00:23,345
She canceled our hike because it might rain. I replied, "Fair enough." Then she asked, "Are you mad?"

4
00:00:23,345 --> 00:00:32,750
Ah. You accepted her reason, but your message may have sounded like the conversation was closed.

5
00:00:32,750 --> 00:00:40,656
Exactly. I thought "fair enough" meant, "That is completely fine, and I support you."

6
00:00:40,656 --> 00:00:48,563
It can be friendly, but it often means something smaller: "I accept your reason."

7
00:00:48,563 --> 00:00:51,609
Accept is not the same as agree.

8
00:00:51,609 --> 00:00:58,443
Right. And that little gap is where the whole feeling of this phrase lives.

9
00:00:58,443 --> 00:01:10,212
Good. Today I need the difference between "fair enough," "that's fair," and "fair point" before I lose another hiking partner.

10
00:01:10,212 --> 00:01:20,762
By the end, you will know which one accepts, which one validates, and which one admits the other person has a good argument.

11
00:01:20,762 --> 00:01:27,598
So start with the basic question. What does "fair enough" actually mean?

12
00:01:27,598 --> 00:01:34,576
Most often, it means, "Your reason is reasonable, so I will accept it."

13
00:01:34,576 --> 00:01:41,279
Reasonable means the reason is good enough, even if it is not my favorite answer.

14
00:01:41,279 --> 00:01:49,557
Exactly. Imagine I say, "I cannot meet tonight. I have an early flight tomorrow."

15
00:01:49,557 --> 00:01:54,104
I could answer, "Fair enough. Let's meet next week."

16
00:01:54,104 --> 00:02:01,653
Perfect. You may still want to meet, but you understand why tonight does not work.

17
00:02:01,653 --> 00:02:09,151
So there is a tiny feeling of, "I would prefer something else, but okay."

18
00:02:09,151 --> 00:02:15,269
Sometimes, yes. That is why it can sound slightly reluctant.

19
00:02:15,269 --> 00:02:21,460
Reluctant means I accept it, but I am not excited about it.

20
00:02:21,460 --> 00:02:28,724
Right. Your answer moves forward, but it does not throw a party.

21
00:02:28,724 --> 00:02:37,631
That describes my text exactly. No smile, no next plan, just "fair enough."

22
00:02:37,631 --> 00:02:46,896
In a text, those two words alone can feel final because the listener cannot hear your warm voice.

23
00:02:46,896 --> 00:02:54,956
So the phrase was not rude. It was just easy to read as cold.

24
00:02:54,956 --> 00:03:02,507
Yes. Context, tone, and what you say next all change it.

25
00:03:02,507 --> 00:03:06,137
Give me the grammar before we get into the feelings.

26
00:03:06,137 --> 00:03:13,683
"Fair enough" is a fixed phrase. Do not change it to "fairly enough" or "enough fair."

27
00:03:13,683 --> 00:03:17,250
Please tell me nobody says "enough fair."

28
00:03:17,250 --> 00:03:25,662
Learners build it from normal adjective order, and English rewards them with a phrase nobody uses.

29
00:03:25,662 --> 00:03:31,425
Wonderful. So I memorize the whole chunk: "Fair enough."

30
00:03:31,425 --> 00:03:39,688
Yes. Save it as one piece. It can stand alone or come before another sentence.

31
00:03:39,688 --> 00:03:44,393
Like, "Fair enough. We can leave at eight instead."

32
00:03:44,393 --> 00:03:49,819
Exactly. The second sentence makes your intention clearer.

33
00:03:49,819 --> 00:03:52,875
Can I say, "That is fair enough"?

34
00:03:52,875 --> 00:04:03,285
You can, but it may mean something different. It can mean something is acceptable, though not excellent.

35
00:04:03,285 --> 00:04:09,127
Give me an example, because I can already feel the meanings fighting.

36
00:04:09,127 --> 00:04:17,676
"The hotel room was small, but it was fair enough for one night." That means it was acceptable.

37
00:04:17,676 --> 00:04:28,675
So "fair enough" by itself accepts a reason, but "the room was fair enough" judges the room as okay.

38
00:04:28,675 --> 00:04:38,438
Exactly. The second use is less common in everyday American conversation, but you may still hear it.

39
00:04:38,438 --> 00:04:43,146
For today, the useful version is the response.

40
00:04:43,146 --> 00:04:50,553
Yes. Someone explains, you consider it, and you say, "Fair enough."

41
00:04:50,553 --> 00:04:54,682
Now give me a situation where it sounds warm.

42
00:04:54,682 --> 00:05:01,374
Your friend says, "I am skipping dinner because I need to finish a project."

43
00:05:01,374 --> 00:05:07,710
I say, "Fair enough. Good luck with it, and let's catch up this weekend."

44
00:05:07,710 --> 00:05:14,974
Warm and easy. You accepted the reason and kept the relationship open.

45
00:05:14,974 --> 00:05:19,021
The extra sentence does a lot of emotional work.

46
00:05:19,021 --> 00:05:27,859
It does. "Fair enough" handles the logic. Your next sentence can handle the person.

47
00:05:27,859 --> 00:05:32,118
Logic first, person second. I like that.

48
00:05:32,118 --> 00:05:39,095
Another warm example: "Fair enough. I would probably do the same thing."

49
00:05:39,095 --> 00:05:44,088
That sounds much more supportive than my lonely little period.

50
00:05:44,088 --> 00:05:49,634
Punctuation can have a personality in text messages.

51
00:05:49,634 --> 00:05:55,184
What about a casual disagreement? Suppose you hate a movie I love.

52
00:05:55,184 --> 00:06:00,165
I might say, "The ending was too slow for me."

53
00:06:00,165 --> 00:06:08,002
And I answer, "Fair enough. I loved the slow ending, but I get why you did not."

54
00:06:08,002 --> 00:06:16,267
That is a great use. You are not changing your opinion. You are accepting mine as reasonable.

55
00:06:16,267 --> 00:06:22,314
So "fair enough" protects two different opinions in the same room.

56
00:06:22,314 --> 00:06:27,574
Exactly. It says, "We do not need to fight about this."

57
00:06:27,574 --> 00:06:30,622
That sounds very useful online.

58
00:06:30,622 --> 00:06:36,884
In theory. The internet has not fully accepted the offer.

59
00:06:36,884 --> 00:06:40,022
Is "fair enough" common at work?

60
00:06:40,022 --> 00:06:49,497
Yes, especially in relaxed conversations with coworkers. But it can sound a little short with a manager or client.

61
00:06:49,497 --> 00:06:56,262
Because it sounds like I am judging whether their reason passes my test?

62
00:06:56,262 --> 00:07:02,241
That is the risk. You are saying their explanation is acceptable to you.

63
00:07:02,241 --> 00:07:09,364
Which is fine with a teammate, but strange if my boss says the office is closed Friday.

64
00:07:09,364 --> 00:07:20,056
Right. Your boss does not need your approval. "Got it" or "that makes sense" may fit better.

65
00:07:20,056 --> 00:07:28,981
So if a coworker says, "I moved the meeting because Sam is out," I can say, "Fair enough."

66
00:07:28,981 --> 00:07:36,816
Yes, though "That makes sense" sounds a little warmer and more cooperative.

67
00:07:36,816 --> 00:07:41,078
Cooperative means it feels like we are working together.

68
00:07:41,078 --> 00:07:51,781
Exactly. "Fair enough" can finish a discussion. "That makes sense" often keeps it flowing.

69
00:07:51,781 --> 00:07:56,132
I think we just found the first big contrast.

70
00:07:56,132 --> 00:08:07,541
We did. "Fair enough" says, "I accept that." "That makes sense" says, "I understand the logic."

71
00:08:07,541 --> 00:08:11,517
Those are close, but not identical.

72
00:08:11,517 --> 00:08:24,927
Right. If I explain why the deadline changed, both can work. If I share a painful feeling, "that makes sense" is usually kinder.

73
00:08:24,927 --> 00:08:30,687
Because a feeling does not need to be approved like an application.

74
00:08:30,687 --> 00:08:38,666
Exactly. Please do not stamp your friend's sadness with "fair enough" and send it back.

75
00:08:38,666 --> 00:08:47,001
Noted. Now what about "that's fair"? That sounds almost identical.

76
00:08:47,001 --> 00:08:54,411
"That's fair" usually means, "Your point, feeling, or request is valid."

77
00:08:54,411 --> 00:08:59,101
Valid means it deserves to be taken seriously.

78
00:08:59,101 --> 00:09:05,796
Yes. It often shows more agreement or empathy than "fair enough."

79
00:09:05,796 --> 00:09:14,205
Let us return to the canceled hike. She says, "I do not want to drive in heavy rain."

80
00:09:14,205 --> 00:09:22,184
"That's fair" says her concern is understandable. "Fair enough" says you accept her decision.

81
00:09:22,184 --> 00:09:28,823
So one looks at her concern, and the other looks at the final decision.

82
00:09:28,823 --> 00:09:32,369
That is a very useful way to hear it.

83
00:09:32,369 --> 00:09:34,389
Which one would you send?

84
00:09:34,389 --> 00:09:42,653
I would write, "That's fair. The weather looks rough. Want to try Sunday instead?"

85
00:09:42,653 --> 00:09:47,643
Warmer, clearer, and it includes a new plan.

86
00:09:47,643 --> 00:09:55,477
Exactly. If I wrote "fair enough," I would add something after it for the same reason.

87
00:09:55,477 --> 00:10:01,525
Can "that's fair" also mean somebody treated people equally?

88
00:10:01,525 --> 00:10:11,790
Yes. The literal meaning of fair is equal or just. "Everyone gets ten minutes. That's fair."

89
00:10:11,790 --> 00:10:16,553
Just means morally right or equal in that example.

90
00:10:16,553 --> 00:10:26,391
Right. But in conversation, "that's fair" often means, "I can see why you feel that way."

91
00:10:26,391 --> 00:10:28,937
Give me a relationship example.

92
00:10:28,937 --> 00:10:35,627
Someone says, "I was upset because you canceled and did not text me."

93
00:10:35,627 --> 00:10:40,389
I should say, "That's fair. I should have told you earlier."

94
00:10:40,389 --> 00:10:46,509
Perfect. You are recognizing that their complaint is valid.

95
00:10:46,509 --> 00:10:54,127
If I say, "Fair enough," it sounds like I am reviewing their complaint from a judge's chair.

96
00:10:54,127 --> 00:11:03,972
It can. Not always, but in an emotional conversation, that distance may feel cold.

97
00:11:03,972 --> 00:11:09,805
So "that's fair" is better when I want to take responsibility.

98
00:11:09,805 --> 00:11:17,617
Often, yes. Add a clear action: "That's fair. I will text you next time."

99
00:11:17,617 --> 00:11:20,234
What about "that's a fair point"?

100
00:11:20,234 --> 00:11:26,640
That means one part of the other person's argument is strong or reasonable.

101
00:11:26,640 --> 00:11:32,992
An argument here means a reasoned opinion, not two people yelling.

102
00:11:32,992 --> 00:11:39,397
Correct. Imagine we are discussing whether a meeting should be online.

103
00:11:39,397 --> 00:11:43,658
I say, "People who commute would save an hour."

104
00:11:43,658 --> 00:11:50,222
I answer, "That's a fair point. An online meeting would save time."

105
00:11:50,222 --> 00:11:52,984
Am I agreeing with the whole plan?

106
00:11:52,984 --> 00:12:01,532
Not necessarily. You may accept that one point and still disagree with the final decision.

107
00:12:01,532 --> 00:12:11,656
Sneaky. So "fair point" can be a small area of agreement inside a bigger disagreement.

108
00:12:11,656 --> 00:12:18,785
Exactly. It is useful when you want to show you listened before adding another view.

109
00:12:18,785 --> 00:12:24,406
Like, "Fair point, but some people may need to be in the room."

110
00:12:24,406 --> 00:12:30,813
Yes. And notice that "but" tells us more is coming.

111
00:12:30,813 --> 00:12:35,577
Does "fair enough" ever come before "but"?

112
00:12:35,577 --> 00:12:47,419
It can. "Fair enough, but I still think we should wait." That accepts the reason without changing your position.

113
00:12:47,419 --> 00:12:52,040
That sounds calm, but also a little firm.

114
00:12:52,040 --> 00:12:59,734
Exactly. Firm means clear and not easy to move, without being rude.

115
00:12:59,734 --> 00:13:06,142
So tone matters again. Can we hear the three versions as text?

116
00:13:06,142 --> 00:13:19,430
Sure. "Fair enough." Neutral acceptance. "That's fair." More validation. "Fair point." One good part of an argument.

117
00:13:19,430 --> 00:13:25,983
Acceptance, validation, argument. That is the map.

118
00:13:25,983 --> 00:13:35,395
And no phrase is automatically warm or cold. Your voice and next sentence still matter.

119
00:13:35,395 --> 00:13:41,657
Let us talk about voice, because learners do not always hear the hidden message.

120
00:13:41,657 --> 00:13:49,217
With a relaxed falling tone, "Fair enough" sounds calm and complete.

121
00:13:49,217 --> 00:13:54,622
It says, "Okay, I understand. We can move on."

122
00:13:54,622 --> 00:14:01,026
Right. With a sharp tone, it can sound annoyed: "Fair enough."

123
00:14:01,026 --> 00:14:09,218
Same words, but now it means, "Fine. I do not want to discuss this anymore."

124
00:14:09,218 --> 00:14:16,925
Exactly. And with a light, rising tone, it can sound playful or surprised.

125
00:14:16,925 --> 00:14:23,063
Like you tell me you wake up at five to bake bread, and I say, "Fair enough?"

126
00:14:23,063 --> 00:14:31,540
That rising tone sounds less natural there. You might say, "Okay, fair enough," with a small laugh.

127
00:14:31,540 --> 00:14:34,883
So "okay" can soften it.

128
00:14:34,883 --> 00:14:43,863
Yes. "Okay, fair enough" shows you considered the explanation and changed your response.

129
00:14:43,863 --> 00:14:46,767
What about "Well, fair enough"?

130
00:14:46,767 --> 00:14:56,604
"Well" often adds a feeling of reluctant acceptance. You had one idea, then the new information changed it.

131
00:14:56,604 --> 00:14:58,624
Example, please.

132
00:14:58,624 --> 00:15:06,028
You say, "Why did you take a taxi?" I say, "The last train had already left."

133
00:15:06,028 --> 00:15:15,151
"Well, fair enough." Meaning, "I questioned you, but now your choice makes sense."

134
00:15:15,151 --> 00:15:19,696
Exactly. It can be friendly, especially with a smile.

135
00:15:19,696 --> 00:15:21,747
And without a smile?

136
00:15:21,747 --> 00:15:28,153
It may sound like you are ending the conversation because you have no answer left.

137
00:15:28,153 --> 00:15:33,647
Text messages are dangerous little rooms with no faces in them.

138
00:15:33,647 --> 00:15:41,624
Which is why one extra sentence can save you: "Fair enough. Hope you get some rest."

139
00:15:41,624 --> 00:15:46,825
Let us do mistakes. I want the traps before we practice.

140
00:15:46,825 --> 00:15:54,232
Mistake one is using "fair enough" when somebody shares a serious feeling.

141
00:15:54,232 --> 00:16:00,782
My friend says, "I felt ignored at dinner," and I say, "Fair enough."

142
00:16:00,782 --> 00:16:10,621
Too distant. Try, "That's fair. I am sorry. I should have included you."

143
00:16:10,621 --> 00:16:12,097
Mistake two?

144
00:16:12,097 --> 00:16:22,792
Using it as an excited yes to a plan. If I ask, "Want to see a concert?" "Fair enough" sounds unexcited.

145
00:16:22,792 --> 00:16:29,268
Better answers are "I'd love to," "sounds great," or "I'm down."

146
00:16:29,268 --> 00:16:38,387
Exactly. "Fair enough" normally responds to a reason or point, not a fun invitation.

147
00:16:38,387 --> 00:16:42,722
Mistake three must be "enough fair."

148
00:16:42,722 --> 00:16:51,487
It is. Keep the phrase in the fixed order: fair first, enough second.

149
00:16:51,487 --> 00:16:53,507
Mistake four?

150
00:16:53,507 --> 00:17:00,913
Saying it to someone who does not need your permission, especially in a formal setting.

151
00:17:00,913 --> 00:17:08,180
The client says, "We need the legal team to review this," and I answer, "Fair enough."

152
00:17:08,180 --> 00:17:16,016
It is not terrible, but "Of course" or "That makes sense" sounds more respectful.

153
00:17:16,016 --> 00:17:23,639
And mistake five is sending only those words when the relationship needs warmth.

154
00:17:23,639 --> 00:17:32,973
Exactly. Add care, a next step, or a small sign that you are still connected.

155
00:17:32,973 --> 00:17:40,598
Time for the game. Give me a situation, and I will choose the best phrase.

156
00:17:40,598 --> 00:17:47,433
Your roommate says, "I cannot clean tonight because I have a final exam tomorrow."

157
00:17:47,433 --> 00:17:52,841
"Fair enough. I can do the kitchen tonight, and you can take Saturday."

158
00:17:52,841 --> 00:17:58,675
Great. You accept the reason and make a practical plan.

159
00:17:58,675 --> 00:18:00,695
Next one.

160
00:18:00,695 --> 00:18:07,241
Your partner says, "I felt nervous when you did not answer for six hours."

161
00:18:07,241 --> 00:18:11,291
"That's fair. I should have sent a quick message."

162
00:18:11,291 --> 00:18:16,841
Perfect. You validate the feeling and take responsibility.

163
00:18:16,841 --> 00:18:19,459
Give me a work disagreement.

164
00:18:19,459 --> 00:18:27,292
A coworker says, "If we launch Friday, nobody will be here to fix problems over the weekend."

165
00:18:27,292 --> 00:18:31,339
"That's a fair point. Monday may be safer."

166
00:18:31,339 --> 00:18:36,173
Exactly. You recognize the strength of the argument.

167
00:18:36,173 --> 00:18:38,577
Now try to trick me.

168
00:18:38,577 --> 00:18:43,268
Your friend says, "Want to get ice cream right now?"

169
00:18:43,268 --> 00:18:49,326
Not "fair enough." I say, "Absolutely. Let's go."

170
00:18:49,326 --> 00:18:56,032
Good. There was no reason to accept, just a beautiful invitation.

171
00:18:56,032 --> 00:18:58,052
Another trick.

172
00:18:58,052 --> 00:19:05,886
Your manager says, "The budget has already been approved, so we cannot change it this week."

173
00:19:05,886 --> 00:19:09,732
"That makes sense. Can we review it next month?"

174
00:19:09,732 --> 00:19:17,138
Strong choice. It sounds cooperative and keeps the discussion open.

175
00:19:17,138 --> 00:19:19,540
One more emotional one.

176
00:19:19,540 --> 00:19:28,373
Your sister says, "I need a quiet weekend. I have been around people all week."

177
00:19:28,373 --> 00:19:33,709
"That's fair. Rest this weekend, and call me when you feel ready."

178
00:19:33,709 --> 00:19:38,542
Warm, clear, and no accidental judgment.

179
00:19:38,542 --> 00:19:42,875
I think I finally hear the center of this phrase.

180
00:19:42,875 --> 00:19:46,136
Say it in your own words.

181
00:19:46,136 --> 00:19:57,116
"Fair enough" does not mean, "I love your answer." It means, "Your reason is reasonable, and I accept it."

182
00:19:57,116 --> 00:20:02,809
Exactly. That is the one sentence to remember.

183
00:20:02,809 --> 00:20:10,226
"That's fair" gives more support to a feeling, request, or complaint.

184
00:20:10,226 --> 00:20:16,776
And "fair point" recognizes one strong idea in a discussion.

185
00:20:16,776 --> 00:20:23,196
If my words feel too short, I add a warm sentence or a next step.

186
00:20:23,196 --> 00:20:35,178
"Fair enough. Let's try Sunday." "That's fair. I should have texted." "Fair point. Monday is safer."

187
00:20:35,178 --> 00:20:39,513
Three tiny phrases, three different jobs.

188
00:20:39,513 --> 00:20:47,063
Your homework is to listen for them this week and notice what comes right after each one.

189
00:20:47,063 --> 00:20:54,826
Then write one example in the comments: a reason you would answer with "fair enough."

190
00:20:54,826 --> 00:21:02,949
If this cleared up a phrase you thought you already knew, subscribe for more real B1 English.

191
00:21:02,949 --> 00:21:09,784
And for the record, I texted my friend again. We moved the hike to Sunday.

192
00:21:09,784 --> 00:21:15,190
Sensible. The trail will still be there.

193
00:21:15,190 --> 00:21:17,210
Fair enough.

194
00:21:17,210 --> 00:21:25,757
Warm voice, no lonely period, and a new plan. You have learned everything.

195
00:21:25,757 --> 00:21:30,025
That is a fair point. Bye, everyone.

196
00:21:30,025 --> 00:21:32,086
See you next time.
